// Auto-generated from legacy HTML by scripts/extract-pages.py
export const title = "Event Meta Token & Action Message Guide";
export const html = "<h1>Event Meta Token & Action Message Guide</h1>\n      <hr class=\"rule\" />\n\n<p>Network-based action handlers let you send messages built from a range of event metadata, such as the <strong>event name</strong> and <strong>time the event occurred</strong>.</p>\n<p>When you configure an action handler that sends a message from the device, you compose the outgoing action message yourself in an editable text field.</p>\n<p>The various event meta tokens available while editing the message let you easily drop dynamic event metadata into the action message.</p>\n<p>Using this kind of action handler, you can hand-write a high-flexibility protocol tailored to whatever device or server you want to integrate with &mdash; without any separate development work, regardless of that target's protocol.</p>\n\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:640px;\">\n<div class=\"app-panel\">\n  <div class=\"form-grid\" style=\"grid-template-columns:150px 1fr auto;\">\n    <span>String Construction</span><select disabled><option>Use template</option></select><span class=\"btn small primary\">Use</span>\n    <span></span><select disabled><option>Select to add tokens</option></select><span class=\"btn small primary\">Add</span>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"margin-top:10px;\">\n    <div style=\"font-size:11px;color:var(--text-muted);margin-bottom:4px;\">Editable Box</div>\n    <div style=\"background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;padding:10px;font-size:12.5px;font-family:monospace;\">CH{{CH}} - {{EVENT NAME}} - {{TIMESTAMP}}</div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"margin-top:10px;\">\n    <div style=\"font-size:11px;color:var(--text-muted);margin-bottom:4px;\">Message Example</div>\n    <div style=\"background:#f7f8fb;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;padding:10px;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--text-muted);\">CH3 - My Event Name - 1561961100.123000</div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"margin-top:10px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;\">\n    <span style=\"font-size:12px;\">Send example message</span><span class=\"btn small primary\">Test</span>\n  </div>\n</div>\n<figcaption>Action message editor</figcaption>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Action Message Editor Components</h2>\n<p>The action message editor UI consists of a <strong>template control</strong>, a <strong>token control</strong>, an <strong>editable box</strong>, an <strong>example box</strong>, and a <strong>Test button</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">Editable Box, Example Box, and Test Button</h3>\n<p>You generally type the message you want to send directly into the editable box. The message you enter can include event metadata tokens in <code>{{XXX}}</code> form. The list of available event metadata tokens appears in the token control's dropdown.</p>\n<p>The example box shows the action message from the editable box with each metadata token replaced by metadata from a sample event. Plain text outside of tokens is shown unchanged, so the example box previews data similar to what's actually sent.</p>\n<p>Clicking the Test button actually sends the sample action message shown in the example box, so you can test the integration against whatever receiver you're configuring.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">Template Control</h3>\n<p>The template control lets you drop a pre-built, template-form action message straight into the editable box.</p>\n\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:420px;\">\n<div class=\"app-panel\">\n  <div class=\"form-grid\" style=\"grid-template-columns:1fr auto;\">\n    <select disabled><option>Use template</option></select><span class=\"btn small primary\">Use</span>\n  </div>\n  <ul style=\"list-style:none;padding:0;margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--text-muted);line-height:2.1;\">\n    <li>Basic Information</li><li>Basic Information (JSON)</li><li>Basic Information + Objects</li>\n    <li>Basic Information + Objects (JSON)</li><li>Basic Information + LPR</li><li>Basic Information + LPR (JSON)</li><li>JSON Pretty (Old)</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Click the item you want from the list of available templates, then click the <strong>Use</strong> button on the right.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--green);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">Using a template message replaces everything in the editable box. If you have work in progress, using a template will discard it &mdash; be careful when using this control.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">Token Control</h3>\n<p>The token control lets you insert event metadata into the action message.</p>\n<p>Select the item you want to configure from the dropdown of available tokens, then click the button on the right. The selected event metadata token is added to the editable box, and the sample-event-substituted metadata appears in the example box.</p>\n<p>The inserted token text can be moved anywhere you like within the editable box.</p>\n<p>The list of supported tokens and details for each are described further down this page.</p>\n\n<h2>Using the {{IF Statement}} Conditional Token</h2>\n<p>Using the conditional token among the event metadata tokens lets you output a sentence only when the given statement is satisfied.</p>\n<p>It must be used in the form <code>{{IF Statement}} Context {{FI}}</code>. <code>{{IF Statement}}</code> and <code>{{FI}}</code> mark the start and end of the conditional block, respectively.</p>\n<p>Content between them is only shown when the condition is true; if the condition isn't met, that portion is skipped.</p>\n<p>Whether the context is shown is decided by the statement's state at the moment the event occurs. The condition can use the Arm/Disarm state or the Event Type.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">Rules for Conditional Tokens</h3>\n<ul class=\"steps\">\n<li><code>{{IF XXX}}</code> and <code>{{FI}}</code> must appear in pairs.</li>\n<li>They can be combined with other event metadata tokens, e.g. <code>{{IF XXX}} {{CH NAME}} {{FI}}</code>.</li>\n<li>They can also be used together with object tokens.</li>\n<li>Conditional tokens cannot be nested. The following is not valid: <code>{{IF AAA}} {{IF BBB}} {{FI}} {{FI}}</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When adding an <strong>IF EVENT TYPE</strong> token, a combo box appears letting you choose the event type.</p>\n\n<h2>Using the {{::OBJ[XXX]}} Object Token</h2>\n<p>Among the event metadata tokens, tokens in the <code>{{::OBJ[XXX]}}</code> form follow a specific set of rules that other tokens don't. These tokens represent various pieces of information about the object or objects that triggered the event.</p>\n<p>A single event can include multiple objects, and any token representing object information is repeated once per object.</p>\n<p>Because of this, object information tokens must be wrapped in a separate list token that marks where the repeated block starts and ends.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">Rules for the OBJ Token</h3>\n<ul class=\"steps\">\n<li>Every <code>{{::OBJ[XXX]}}</code> token must sit between a pair of <code>{{LIST OBJECTS}}</code> or <code>{{LIST OBJECTS[PARAM=COMMA]}}</code> tokens. The first LIST token marks the start of the repeated block, the second marks the end.</li>\n<li>The <code>{{LIST OBJECTS}}</code> / <code>{{LIST OBJECTS[PARAM=COMMA]}}</code> tokens are needed for the syntax to mark a list &mdash; the token itself isn't replaced with any content. Any line break immediately before or after it is preserved in the sent message, so be careful when composing the message.</li>\n<li>A list started with <code>{{LIST OBJECTS}}</code> must be closed with <code>{{LIST OBJECTS}}</code>, and one started with <code>{{LIST OBJECTS[PARAM=COMMA]}}</code> must be closed with the same variant.</li>\n<li>Content wrapped in <code>{{LIST OBJECTS}}</code> has no separator between items &mdash; the wrapped text simply repeats.</li>\n<li>Content wrapped in <code>{{LIST OBJECTS[PARAM=COMMA]}}</code> is separated by a comma (,) between items.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Event Metadata Token Reference</h2>\n<p>The supported event metadata tokens are described below. They fall into three groups: information about the event source and the event itself, information about when the event occurred, and information about the object(s) that triggered the event.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">Event Source &amp; Event Information</h3>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--border);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<ul class=\"steps\" style=\"margin:0;\">\n<li><strong>{{CH}}</strong> &ndash; the channel number the event occurred on (1&ndash;8).</li>\n<li><strong>{{CH NAME}}</strong> &ndash; the name of the channel the event occurred on, as set under video stream settings.</li>\n<li><strong>{{MAC}}</strong> &ndash; the device's MAC address.</li>\n<li><strong>{{DEVICE NAME}}</strong> &ndash; the device name shown at the top left of the UI. Characters up to however much of the MAC address is configured.</li>\n<li><strong>{{RULE NO}}</strong> &ndash; the ID of the action rule this event belongs to.</li>\n<li><strong>{{RULE NAME}}</strong> &ndash; the name of the action rule this event belongs to.</li>\n<li><strong>{{EVENT NAME}}</strong> &ndash; the event's name.</li>\n<li><strong>{{EVENT TYPE}}</strong> &ndash; the event type.</li>\n<li><strong>{{EVENT TYPE[EN]}}</strong> &ndash; always the English EVENT_TYPE value, regardless of the configured UI language.</li>\n<li><strong>{{# OF OBJECTS}}</strong> &ndash; the number of objects in the event.</li>\n<li><strong>{{COMBINED COUNT}}</strong> &ndash; for counting-app events (Occupancy, People Counting, Vehicle Counting, Advanced Visitor Analytics), the target counter's cumulative count at the moment the event fired.</li>\n<li><strong>{{EVENT META}}</strong> &ndash; <strong>{{EVENT META1}}</strong>, <strong>{{EVENT META2}}</strong>, and <strong>{{EVENT META3}}</strong> joined with spaces. See <a href=\"#\">Event Metadata by App</a> for details.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">Event Time Tokens</h3>\n<p>Assuming an event occurred at 18:43:09.739 on March 7, 2023 in the GMT+9:00 time zone, each time token is substituted as follows.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--border);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<ul class=\"steps\" style=\"margin:0;\">\n<li><strong>{{TIME YYYY-MM-DD}}</strong> &ndash; event date, e.g. 2023-03-07</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIME YYYYMMDD}}</strong> &ndash; event date, e.g. 20230307</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIME DD/MM/YYYY}}</strong> &ndash; event date, e.g. 07/03/2023</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIME YYYY}}</strong> &ndash; 4-digit year, e.g. 2023</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIME YY}}</strong> &ndash; 2-digit year, e.g. 23</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIME mm}}</strong> &ndash; 2-digit month, e.g. 03</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIME dd}}</strong> &ndash; 2-digit day, e.g. 07</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIME HH}}</strong> &ndash; hour, 24-hour format, e.g. 18</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIME MM}}</strong> &ndash; 2-digit minute, e.g. 43</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIME SS}}</strong> &ndash; 2-digit second, e.g. 09</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIME MS}}</strong> &ndash; milliseconds, e.g. 739</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIMESTAMP}}</strong> &ndash; Unix timestamp, e.g. 1678182189.739000</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIME ISO8601}}</strong> &ndash; ISO 8601 format, e.g. 2023-03-07T18:43:09.739000+09:00</li>\n<li><strong>{{UTC ISO8601}}</strong> &ndash; UTC time in ISO 8601 format, e.g. 2023-03-07T09:43:09.739000+00:00</li>\n<li><strong>{{TIME}}</strong> &ndash; event time in a fixed display format, e.g. 07 March 2023 18:43:09</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">Conditional Tokens</h3>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--border);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<ul class=\"steps\" style=\"margin:0;\">\n<li><strong>{{IF DISARM EVENT[arm]}}</strong> &ndash; start of an IF block; shows the enclosed text only for a disarm event whose disarm state value is arm.</li>\n<li><strong>{{IF DISARM EVENT[disarm]}}</strong> &ndash; start of an IF block; shows the enclosed text only for a disarm event whose disarm state value is disarm.</li>\n<li><strong>{{IF EVENT TYPE[TYPE]}}</strong> &ndash; start of an IF block; shows the enclosed text only when the Event Type matches TYPE, e.g. <code>{{IF EVENT TYPE[loitering]}}</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>{{FI}}</strong> &ndash; end of an {{IF}} block. Every IF token must be paired with a FI token.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">Object Information Tokens</h3>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--border);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<ul class=\"steps\" style=\"margin:0;\">\n<li><strong>{{LIST OBJECTS}} ~ {{LIST OBJECTS}}</strong> &ndash; repeats the enclosed content once per object.</li>\n<li><strong>{{LIST OBJECTS[PARAM=COMMA]}} ~ {{LIST OBJECTS[PARAM=COMMA]}}</strong> &ndash; repeats the enclosed content once per object, separated by commas.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[INDEX]}}</strong> &ndash; the object's index, starting from 0.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[TRACK ID]}}</strong> &ndash; the object's tracking ID.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[CLASS]}}</strong> &ndash; the object's class. The set of detectable classes varies by app and event type: person / car / bike / violence / fire / abandoned / animal / man / woman / helmet / no-helmet / vest / no-vest / fallen / lp / &hellip;</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[SCORE]}}</strong> &ndash; the object's confidence score. For reference only &mdash; not generally reliable as a standalone basis for judging whether something is a true object.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[BBOX_X1]}}</strong> / <strong>{{::OBJ[BBOX_Y1]}}</strong> &ndash; the top-left X/Y coordinate of the bounding box, normalized 0&ndash;1 (0 = left/top edge, 1 = right/bottom edge).</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[BBOX_X2]}}</strong> / <strong>{{::OBJ[BBOX_Y2]}}</strong> &ndash; the bottom-right X/Y coordinate of the bounding box.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[DIR_LABEL]}}</strong> &ndash; for virtual fence events, the label of the direction the object crossed the fence in.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">LPR Object Information Tokens</h3>\n<p>As with general object information, LPR object information must also be wrapped with <code>{{LIST OBJECTS}}</code> or <code>{{LIST OBJECTS[PARAM=COMMA]}}</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--border);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<ul class=\"steps\" style=\"margin:0;\">\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[LP_TEXT_DETECTED]}}</strong> &ndash; the plate number recognized by LPR.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[LP_TEXT_DB]}}</strong> &ndash; the plate number as registered by the user in the database.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[LP_GROUP_NAME]}}</strong> &ndash; the name of the group the registered plate belongs to. If the plate belongs to multiple groups at once, the names are comma-separated, e.g. Group 1,Group 2.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[LP_ID]}}</strong> &ndash; the DB index number of the registered plate.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[LP_NOTE]}}</strong> &ndash; the note attached to the registered plate.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[LP_COUNTRY_CODE]}}</strong> &ndash; the recognized plate's country code. LPR-EU: 2-letter country code (EU if unrecognized). LPR-US: 2-letter state code (US if unrecognized). LPR-JP: always JP. LPR-KR: always KR.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[MOVEMENT_DIR]}}</strong> &ndash; the recognized plate's direction of travel (shown as A or B).</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[MOVEMENT_DIR_NAME]}}</strong> &ndash; the name configured for that direction on the event.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--green);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">LP_TEXT_DETECTED and LP_TEXT_DB are usually identical, but under a loose matching policy they can still match even when not an exact match.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">Object Attribute Tokens</h3>\n<p>Enabling the Basic Attributes or Advanced Attributes app adds further analysis of detected people's attributes. To include an object's attribute information in an action message, wrap it with <code>{{LIST OBJECTS}}</code> or <code>{{LIST OBJECTS[PARAM=COMMA]}}</code> as with other object information.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--border);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<ul class=\"steps\" style=\"margin:0;\">\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[ATTR_TOP_COLOR]}}</strong> &ndash; top color. One of red, orange, yellow, green, blue, purple, pink, brown, white, gray, black, or blank if ambiguous.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[ATTR_BOTTOM_COLOR]}}</strong> &ndash; bottom color, same value set as above, or blank if ambiguous.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[ATTR_TOP_TYPE]}}</strong> &ndash; top type. One of long_sleeve, short_sleeve, sleeveless, onepiece (includes any one-piece garment such as a long padded coat), or blank if ambiguous.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[ATTR_BOTTM_TYPE]}}</strong> &ndash; bottom type. One of long_pants, short_pants, skirt, none (none is possible when the top is onepiece), or blank if ambiguous.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[ATTR_GENDER]}}</strong> &ndash; gender. One of man, woman, or blank if ambiguous.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[ATTR_AGE]}}</strong> &ndash; age group. One of child, teenager, adult, senior, or blank if ambiguous.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[ATTR_ACCESSORIES]}}</strong> &ndash; accessories. One of carrier, umbrella, bag, hat, glasses, none, or blank if ambiguous.</li>\n<li><strong>{{::OBJ[ATTR_PET]}}</strong> &ndash; whether a pet is present. yes, no, or blank if ambiguous.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">Generic Event Information Tokens</h3>\n<p>These tokens are available when configuring generic events. See the <a href=\"/generic-event-guide\">Generic Event Setup Guide</a> for setup details.</p>\n<p>Assuming a generic event API call of <code>GET /api/generic/event/?eventtype=leak&amp;name=water&amp;description=pipe&amp;ch=1&amp;metadata={{\"assignee\": \"Yeongjun\",\"region\": \"Korea\"}}</code>, each token below is substituted as follows.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--border);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<ul class=\"steps\" style=\"margin:0;\">\n<li><strong>{{GENERIC EVENT TYPE}}</strong> &ndash; the eventtype value from the generic event API, e.g. leak</li>\n<li><strong>{{GENERIC EVENT NAME}}</strong> &ndash; the name value, e.g. water</li>\n<li><strong>{{GENERIC EVENT DESCRIPTION}}</strong> &ndash; the description value, e.g. pipe</li>\n<li><strong>{{GENERIC EVENT METADATA}}</strong> &ndash; the metadata value, e.g. {{\"assignee\": \"Yeongjun\",\"region\": \"Korea\"}}</li>\n</ul>";
